      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The evaluation of needs and means concerning the care of patients in palliative treatment is among the problems seen as priority by the High Committee for Public Health. We have thus tried to characterise, in a specific health sector, the patients receiving palliative care in short-stay establishments in order to evaluate their care needs. We carried out an exhaustive descriptive survey among all public and private short-stay establishments in the Côte d'Or region. Of the 2116 patients in the hospital on the day of the survey, roughly 30% were considered as eligible for palliative care (patients suffering from serious, chronic and progressive illnesses). The average age of these patients is 63.9 years (standard deviation 19.7). They mainly suffer from tumours (50%), circulatory pathologies (15%), mental illness (7%), or neurological illnesses (6%). Among the patients that may need palliative care, 38% say they feel pain despite treatment with pain-killers among 25.3% of them. The personnel providing care is insufficiently trained in palliative care or in pain, as only a maximum of 18% of nurses and 5% of doctors in short-stay establishments have been trained in these areas."},
